# Pixelify Sans: the glyphs Blip draws, composes or corrects. SIL Open Font License 1.1; the font is
# Copyright 2021 The Pixelify Sans Project Authors (OFL-PixelifySans.txt). The build applies these to the
# unmodified font in vendor/ and says so in the font's version string.
#
# Why: the upstream font draws its accents, quotes, commas, small figures and some symbols in half pixels or finer,
# which blur into grey at text sizes (its @ reads as a dot, © as °); it misdraws some Greek and Cyrillic letters and
# leaves some out. Everything here is drawn on the font's own pixels, so it matches the letters at every weight.
#
# DRAWING. One block per glyph: a header '@ <char>' (or '@ U+XXXX', or '@ <glyph name>' for a glyph with no
# character), then its rows: 'X' is a pixel, '.' is empty, top row first. A pixel is Pixelify's own (about 91
# units); the build puts every edge where the font's letters have one on that line, at regular and at bold. The
# bottom row sits on the baseline unless the header says 'base=N' (the row it sits on: capitals are rows 0 to 6, the
# x-height is rows 0 to 4, -1 is the first row below the baseline). 'left=N' starts it N columns right (or left, if
# negative) of where letters start; 'lift=N' raises it N units (the period and its family sit 12 units above the
# letters' rows). A glyph is as wide as its rows, and the font adds its side bearing after it.
#
# MARKS. 'mark=above' draws a combining accent as it sits over the x-height (rows 6 and 7, one empty row above the
# letter); over a capital or a tall letter the build lifts it two rows. 'mark=below' hangs under the letter's middle,
# 'mark=ogonek' from its last column, 'mark=beside' (the caron of ď, ľ, ť) next to its stem. A mark that cannot be
# centred to the pixel goes left, or right with 'lean=right'. Every accented letter the font already has follows a
# redrawn mark onto its letter's grid.
#
# COMPOSING. '@ <char> = <letter> <mark>' puts a mark on a letter; 'part(x,y)' places a part x columns right and y
# rows up instead. One part on its own makes the character another name for that glyph ('@ U+041E = O').
#
# Build after editing (pnpm --filter @blip/font build): it fails, naming the glyph, if any glyph of the three
# files still has a stroke thinner than one pixel, at regular or bold, or if a default feature joins letters.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Figures. 5: the upstream 5 is an S; here the top bar ends square and the stem runs straight into the middle bar.

@ 5
XXXX.
X....
X....
XXXX.
....X
X...X
.XXX.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Accents: two rows tall, one pixel thick. The breve is a cup, the caron a point, the double acute two acutes.

@ U+0300 mark=above base=6
X.
.X

@ U+0301 mark=above lean=right base=6
.X
X.

@ U+0302 mark=above base=6
.X.
X.X

@ U+0303 mark=above base=6
.X.X
X.X.

@ U+0304 mark=above base=6
